  "reply": "**No, not this month at the current pace.** Oriel's forecast puts the end of SYN-1 Checkout v2: phone wallets and saved cards around **Jan 14**, and October has two days left.\n\nThis thread's region is empty, so I searched outside it to find the epic and its change log. What I found is read-only.\n\nOriel's caption on the progress chart: *\"Least-squares line over 7 whole weeks, R² 0.94, 0.8 a week. At that rate the 9 left get there around Jan 14 (Dec 21 – Mar 16, 80%), if no more are added.\"*\n\nThe blocks below show where each item stands. What's worth knowing:\n\n- **Four items are in progress:** SYN-21 Update the payment declined error message, SYN-23 Pay with a phone wallet on the website, SYN-92 Show the fare breakdown before payment and SYN-104 Handle 3-D Secure challenges in the native checkout. SYN-21 Update the payment declined error message has been in progress for over three weeks, and SYN-104 Handle 3-D Secure challenges in the native checkout only started yesterday.\n- **Five items haven't started**, and most have been waiting since early September: SYN-10 Remove the legacy web-view checkout, SYN-57 Checkout analytics: drop-off by step, SYN-88 Refund to the original payment method when a sailing is cancelled, SYN-98 Apply promo codes at checkout and SYN-99 Save a card for next time.\n- **Amara Okafor has three of the open items:** SYN-23 Pay with a phone wallet on the website, SYN-92 Show the fare breakdown before payment and SYN-98 Apply promo codes at checkout.\n- **Three open items have no estimate:** SYN-23 Pay with a phone wallet on the website, SYN-99 Save a card for next time and SYN-104 Handle 3-D Secure challenges in the native checkout. That makes the remaining work harder to size.\n\nIf you want to work on this in a thread, I can add the epic and its items to a workstream. There's already one called \"Checkout\" in your window."
}

Claims:
1. The reply gives no finish date, rate or forecast of its own: any it gives is the one Oriel's block caption states, or it says Oriel had too little history to forecast. A yes or no to the question that follows from Oriel's forecast is not one of its own.
